<%# Budget Form Partial - matches Categories design patterns

    POOL-MODE ONLY (plan 3, task 3). This form used to be MODE-AWARE because a rule could belong to
    a category OR a pool, and it served both: a category picker offering a monthly spending cap,
    and a pool branch about how an envelope gets filled. The cap is deleted, so the two category
    arms — the picker for a new one and the read-only name for an existing one — are gone with it,
    along with the `prorated` switch, which spread a monthly cap across the days of the month and
    was already omitted from the pool branch for reading nothing there.

    THE SECOND SHAPE IS A PROPOSAL (§8's suggestion panel, amendment A): the form has an OPTIONAL
    ENVELOPE HALF rather than the accept flow having a second controller. What that half makes
    happen is BudgetProposal's to explain; what matters here is that the form must say which of the
    two things it is about to do — JOIN an envelope the user already has, or MAKE one — because
    those are different acts and the panel sentence one click earlier already named one of them.

    THE THIRD SHAPE IS A HAND-MADE RULE (Henry's ruling of 2026-08-20), and it is what this comment
    used to say could not exist. It read: "A BARE `/budgets/new` NOW RENDERS NO OWNER CONTROL AT
    ALL, and that is the honest state: every link into this form carries an owner… the form says so
    in its error notification rather than offering a picker for a mode that no longer exists." The
    premise was true and the conclusion did not follow — nothing linked to the bare URL because
    there was no button, so "every link carries an owner" was a fact about the links that existed
    rather than about what a user might want. A rule for a bill your entries have not shown yet has
    no other door.

    SO THE OWNER PICKER IS BACK, ON THAT PATH ONLY. It offers `Pool.rule_owners` — this user's
    envelopes and goals, never an account, which is `Budget#pool_must_not_be_an_account` stated as
    a choice instead of a refusal — and `BudgetsController#owner_picker?` is the one decision about
    when it renders. Every path that ALREADY names an owner is untouched: the Edit link's rule, the
    panel's envelope half, the panel's reused `pool_id`.

    A HAND-MADE RULE IS A PER-PERIOD RATE, and `basis` rides hidden saying so. That is the only
    shape this form can build without asking a second question ("$X a period" needs no schedule,
    while §3.1's other three rows all need an interval, an anchor or both), and it is the shape a
    user typing a rule from scratch means: money set aside every period. A DATED bill still arrives
    the way it always has — measured, from the suggestion panel, with its date and interval on
    screen and correctable. %>
<% pool_mode = @budget.pool_mode? %>
<% proposal = @envelope.present? %>
<%# WHETHER THIS FORM IS ASKING WHO OWNS THE RULE. The controller's answer, not a re-derivation:
    "an owner is already in context" is a fact about how the request arrived (a prefill, an envelope
    half, a persisted rule) and cannot be read off the record once the picker's own choice has been
    assigned to it. %>
<% owner_picker = @owner_picker.present? %>
<%# THE ENVELOPE THIS WOULD JOIN, or nil where it would create one. Resolved by
    BudgetsController#set_envelope through BudgetProposal::Envelope#existing — the same reader the
    save uses, so this heading cannot promise something the write will not do. %>
<% joining = proposal && @joined_envelope %>
<%# THE SCHEDULE IS ONLY EDITABLE ON THE WAY IN. A rule's shape (basis, interval, anchor, item) is
    measured by the engine and submitted back; changing it later is the edit form's business, and
    an existing rule's shape is not this partial's to re-offer. %>
<% new_pool_rule = @budget.new_record? && (pool_mode || proposal || owner_picker) %>
<% subtitle = "How an envelope gets filled each period" %>
<% subtitle = "Pick what this rule fills, and how much it asks for each period" if owner_picker %>
<% subtitle = "How #{@budget.pool.name} gets filled each period" if pool_mode && !owner_picker %>
<% subtitle = "A new #{@envelope.name} envelope, and the rule that fills it" if proposal %>
<% subtitle = "Joining your #{joining.name} envelope, and the rule that adds to it" if joining %>
<% cancel_path = budget_page_path %>
<%= page_header(title: @budget.persisted? ? "Edit Budget" : "New Budget", subtitle: subtitle) %>

<div class="max-w-2xl mx-auto">
  <%= simple_form_for @budget, html: { class: "space-y-6" } do |f| %>
    <%= f.error_notification %>
    <%= f.error_notification message: f.object.errors[:base].to_sentence if f.object.errors[:base].present? %>

    <%# Main Form Card %>
    <div class="bg-white shadow-sm rounded border border-gray-100 p-6">
      <div class="space-y-6">
        <% if joining %>
          <%# JOINING AN ENVELOPE THE USER ALREADY HAS. No name field and no account picker: the
              name is not a decision here (renaming it would not make a second envelope, it would
              simply stop matching), and the account is the one the envelope already sits in —
              `BudgetProposal` never reads `envelope[account_id]` on this path, so a picker would
              be a control that changes nothing under a heading that says it does.

              BOTH STILL GO ON THE WIRE, hidden. `Envelope#existing` is resolved again at save
              time, and if the envelope were deleted between this render and the submit the
              creation branch takes over — with the name and the account it was going to need. A
              form that dropped them would answer that race with "Account must be set for budget
              pools" on a screen that never asked for one. %>
          <div>
            <label class="form-label">Envelope</label>
            <div class="form-input flex items-center bg-gray-50">
              <%= joining.name %>
            </div>
            <p class="form-hint">
              You already have this envelope<%= " — funded from #{joining.account.name}" if joining.account %>.
              This rule joins it, and all <%= @envelope.category.name %> spending is pointed at it from now on.
            </p>
            <%# `id: nil` on both: `hidden_field_tag` would otherwise mint `envelope_name` and
                `envelope_account_id`, the same ids the creation branch gives its VISIBLE controls.
                The two branches are mutually exclusive so nothing collides today, but a hidden
                input answering to the id of a control the user is meant to fill in is a trap for
                the next person to open this file — and these two are carriers, not fields. %>
            <%= hidden_field_tag "envelope[name]", joining.name, id: nil %>
            <%= hidden_field_tag "envelope[account_id]", joining.account_id, id: nil %>
          </div>

          <%= hidden_field_tag "envelope[category_id]", @envelope.category.id %>
        <% elsif proposal %>
          <%# THE ENVELOPE HALF. Editable, because both fields can legitimately need changing: the
              proposed name is the CATEGORY's name and the user may already have a pool called
              that (Pool validates name uniqueness per user), and `account_id` is read from
              `users.default_account_id`, which is nullable — a user who has nominated no account
              must be ASKED for one rather than have a blank submitted on their behalf, since
              `account_matches_pool_type` refuses an account-less budget pool outright.

              `envelope[category_id]` is hidden and is NOT the rule's owner: it is the category to
              be re-pointed at the new envelope. See BudgetsController#set_envelope for why it
              cannot travel under the form's own `category_id`. %>
          <div>
            <%= label_tag "envelope_name", "New envelope", class: "form-label" %>
            <%= text_field_tag "envelope[name]", @envelope.name, id: "envelope_name", class: "form-input" %>
            <p class="form-hint">
              Accepting this points <%= @envelope.category.name %> at this envelope, so all
              <%= @envelope.category.name %> spending comes out of it from now on.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <%= label_tag "envelope_account_id", "Funded from", class: "form-label" %>
            <%= select_tag "envelope[account_id]",
                           options_from_collection_for_select(
                             current_user.pools.accounts.order(:name), :id, :name, @envelope.account&.id
                           ),
                           include_blank: "Select an account",
                           id: "envelope_account_id",
                           class: "form-input" %>
            <p class="form-hint">The account this envelope is filled from when you distribute a period's income.</p>
          </div>

          <%= hidden_field_tag "envelope[category_id]", @envelope.category.id %>
        <% elsif owner_picker %>
          <%# THE OWNER, ASKED (Henry's ruling of 2026-08-20) — the one branch of this form where
              the pool is a question rather than something the request arrived carrying.

              `Pool.rule_owners` THROUGH `current_user.pools`, which is both halves of the scoping:
              the model scope answers WHAT KIND of pool may own a rule (an envelope or a goal, never
              an account) and the association answers WHOSE. A foreign id typed into a submit is
              refused a second time on the way in — `BudgetsController#scoped_owner` looks `pool_id`
              up through `current_user.pools` and 404s — so this select is the affordance, not the
              boundary.

              THE BLANK IS OFFERED because "I have not chosen yet" is a real state on a form that
              opens with nothing chosen, not because a rule may have no owner:
              `Budget#must_belong_to_a_pool` refuses the blank on `:base`, which is where this form
              renders base errors. %>
          <div>
            <%= f.input :pool_id,
                as: :select,
                collection: current_user.pools.rule_owners.order(:name),
                label_method: :name,
                value_method: :id,
                include_blank: "Select an envelope or goal",
                label: "Pool",
                hint: "The envelope or goal this rule fills each period." %>
          </div>
        <% elsif pool_mode %>
          <%# THE FORM DOES NOT OFFER THE POOL, which is not the same as the pool being
              unwritable: `budget_params` permits `pool_id`, so a tampered submission DOES
              re-parent the rule — within the user's own pools and nowhere else, because
              `scoped_owner` looks it up through `current_user.pools` and a stranger's id 404s.
              Both directions are pinned in spec/requests/budgets_spec.rb.

              No picker because the page that lists rules groups them BY pool, so moving one
              between envelopes is a re-parenting that list should own rather than a control
              buried in one rule's form. The field is submitted back so the record keeps the
              owner it arrived with. %>
          <div>
            <label class="form-label">Pool</label>
            <div class="form-input flex items-center bg-gray-50">
              <%= @budget.pool.name %>
            </div>
            <%= f.hidden_field :pool_id %>
          </div>
        <% end %>

        <%# Budget Amount %>
        <div>
          <%= f.input :amount,
              as: :decimal,
              label: "Rule Amount",
              input_html: {
                placeholder: "0.00",
                step: 0.01,
                min: 0
              },
              hint: budget_amount_hint(@budget, schedule_shown: new_pool_rule) %>

          <%# WHAT THE RULE SAYS TODAY, beside what a drift suggestion is proposing it should say.
              The prefilled figure is in THE RULE'S OWN UNIT — SuggestionEngine inverts
              `Budget#steady_ask` before putting it on the wire, precisely so that nothing here
              converts it — and the label above already names that unit, so the two figures on
              screen are directly comparable. Shown only when they differ: on every other edit the
              field IS the current amount and repeating it would be one number printed twice. %>
          <% if @current_amount.present? && @budget.amount.to_d != @current_amount.to_d %>
            <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-gray-500" data-current-amount>
              Currently <%= number_to_currency(@current_amount) %> <%= budget_rule_basis(@budget) %>.
            </p>
          <% end %>
        </div>

        <% if new_pool_rule %>
          <%# THE SHAPE OF THE RULE, measured by the suggestion engine and submitted back. `basis`
              rides hidden because it is not a decision the user is being asked to make here — it
              is which of the two rule shapes was detected — while the INTERVAL and the ANCHOR are
              both offered, because both are INFERENCES and the panel says so about one of them
              already. Correcting a guess in the form it came from is cheaper than writing a wrong
              rule and editing it afterwards.

              THE ANCHOR USED TO RIDE HIDDEN BESIDE THE BASIS, and Henry's real-use testing
              (ruling of 2026-08-20) is why it no longer does: `SuggestionEngine#next_due_on` walks
              the LAST PAYMENT forward by the interval, so a bill paid late once proposes a rule
              whose every future due date is late with it — and the amount on the same form was
              editable while the date that decides when the money is needed was not.

              THE PAIR IS STILL RENDERED TOGETHER, and that is the shape rule rather than a
              layout choice: §3.1's row 3 is `monthly + interval + anchor`, and a form offering
              the interval without the anchor (or the reverse) could submit row 2 with an N > 1,
              which `shape_must_be_valid` refuses. A rule with NO anchor — every rate — renders
              neither, and its interval rides hidden. %>
          <%= f.hidden_field :basis %>

          <% if @budget.anchor_date.present? %>
            <div>
              <%= f.input :anchor_date,
                  as: :date,
                  html5: true,
                  label: "First due",
                  hint: "When this bill next comes round. Read from your entries — change it if the last payment was early or late." %>
            </div>

            <div>
              <%= f.input :interval_months,
                  as: :integer,
                  label: "Comes round every (months)",
                  input_html: { min: 1, step: 1 },
                  hint: "Change this if the bill runs on a different cycle." %>
            </div>
          <% else %>
            <%= f.hidden_field :interval_months %>
          <% end %>

          <% if @budget.item %>
            <%# THE ITEM THIS RULE PAYS, shown and submitted back rather than picked. It is what
                makes the rule payable at all — BudgetCalculator reads its entries to decide
                whether the bill has been settled this cycle — and it arrived from the engine,
                which chose it by measuring the item's own history. `item_id` is ownership-scoped
                on the way in (BudgetsController#scoped_owners): a stranger's id would write a
                rule against THEIR spending, so it 404s rather than rendering their item's name
                here. %>
            <div>
              <label class="form-label">Pays</label>
              <div class="form-input flex items-center bg-gray-50">
                <%= @budget.item.name %>
              </div>
              <%= f.hidden_field :item_id %>
            </div>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>

      </div>
    </div>

    <%# Action Buttons %>
    <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row sm:justify-end gap-3">
      <%# Back where the rule is listed, which for a pool-mode rule is the Budget page and never
          the pool page — the same destination BudgetsController sends a save to. %>
      <%= link_to "Cancel", cancel_path, class: "btn btn-secondary" %>

      <%= f.submit @budget.persisted? ? "Update Budget" : "Create Budget",
          class: "btn btn-primary" %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
</div>
